It is not stated in the statement of the problem but we will assume that  $a_i$  is a positive integer for all  $1\leq i \leq n$ .
Without loss of generality let  $a_1 \leq a_2 \leq \dots \leq a_n$ . We will use strong induction on  $n$  to prove that   $(a_1, a_2, \dots, a_n) = (1,  \dots, 1, n+1)$  when  $n$  is even and  $(a_1, a_2, \dots, a_n) = (1,  \dots, 1, n+1)$  or  $(2, 2, \dots, 2)$  when  $n$  is odd. It is easy to check the validity of our claim for small values of  $n$ . So, suppose it is true up to  $n$  and let's prove for  $n$ .**Case 1:** If  $a_1 \geq 2$ , then 
\begin{align}
2n = a_1+a_2+ \cdots +a_n \geq 2 + 2+ \cdots + 2 = 2n \label{first}
\end{align}
which gives us  $(a_1, a_2, \dots, a_n) = (2, 2, \dots, 2)$ . Note that this  $n$ -tuple does not work when  $n$  is even since  $\frac{n}{2}$  of these numbers add up to  $n$ .**Case 2:** If  $a_1=1$ , then let  $$ 1= a_1 =  \dots =a_k  < a_{k+1}=p $$  Note that such  $k$  exists since  $(1, 1, \dots, 1)$  is not a solution. Then  $$ 2n = a_1+a_2+ \cdots +a_n \geq p + \underbrace{1 +  1+ \cdots + 1}_{n-1} = p + n-1 $$  and this gives us  $p\leq n+1$ . If  $p=n+1$ , then we have equality case which can be if and only if   $$ (a_1, a_2, \dots, a_n) = (1, \dots, 1, n+1). $$  Since  $p\neq n$  we are left to analyse the case  $p \leq n-1$ . Note that  $$ 2n = a_1 + a_2 + \cdots + a_n \geq \underbrace{1 + \cdots + 1}_{k} + \underbrace{p + \cdots + p}_{n-k} = k + (n-k)p $$  and this gives us  $$ k \geq \frac{n(p-2)}{p-1} \geq p-2 $$  Then   $$ \underbrace{a_1+ \cdots + a_{p-2} +a_{k+1}}_{2p-2} + a_{i_1} + \cdots + a_{i_{n-p+1}} = 2n $$  for some  $p-2 <i_1 < i_2 < \dots <i_{n-p+1}$  different from  $k+1$ . Thus  $$ a_{i_1} + a_{i_2}  + \cdots + a_{i_{n-p+1}} = 2(n-p+1) $$  **(a)** If  $a_{i_1} \geq 2$ , then as in  $(1)$  we must have  $a_{i_1} = \dots = a_{i_{n-p+1}} = 2$ . Then  $$ a_{k+1} + a_{i_1} + \cdots + a_{i_{\frac{n-p}{2}}} = p + \underbrace{2 + 2 + \dots + 2}_{\frac{n-p}{2}} = n $$  when  $n-p$  is even and   $$ a_1 + a_{k+1} + a_{i_1} + \cdots + a_{i_{\frac{n-p}{2}}} = 1 + p + \underbrace{2 + 2 + \dots + 2}_{\frac{n-p-1}{2}} = n $$  when  $n-p$  is odd. So we get a contradiction in both cases.**(b)** If  $a_{i_1} =1$ , then we claim that no subset of   $$ A=\{a_{i_1}, \dots, a_{i_{n-p+1}}\} $$  add up to  $n-p+1$ . For a contradiction suppose there is a subset  $S \subseteq A$  whose elements sum to  $n-p+1$ .
- if  $a_{i_1} \not\in S$ , then elements of  $S\cup \{a_1, \dots, a_{p-2}, a_{i_1}\}$  sum to  $n$  ,
- if  $a_{i_1} \in S$ , then elements of  $\left(S\setminus \{a_{i_1}\}\right)\cup \{a_{k+1}\}$  sum to  $n$ .
 So in both cases we get a contradiction. Then by induction hypothesis   $$ (a_{i_1}, a_{i_2}, \dots, a_{i_{n-p+1}}) = (\underbrace{1, 1, \dots, 1}_{n-p}, n-p +2). $$  But this is also impossible since   $$ a_{k+1} + a_{i_1}+ a_{i_2} + \cdots + a_{i_{n-p}} = p + \underbrace{1 + 1 + \dots +1}_{n-p} = n $$

Let  $M$  be the middle point of  $BC$ ,  $P$  the middle point of  $DE$ , and  $G$  be the intersection of  $AM$  and  $FP$ .

Consider  $D'$  a point on  $BC$  such that  ${{CD'}/{D'B}}={{BD}/{DC}}$ . It follows that  
\[\frac{BD}{BC} = \frac{CE}{CA}=\frac{AF}{AB}=\frac{CD'}{CB} \hspace{.5 cm}(1)\]
[asy]
