The answer is no. Fix the set of primes  $p \le N$  which we will call  $T_N$  for brevity. We find the sum of the terms with indices divisible by at least one entry in  $T_N$ . Call this sum  $S_N$ . Using the principle of inclusion-exclusion, this is given by \begin{align*}S_N&=\sum_{\substack{ A \subseteq T_N \emptyset \neq A}}  \frac{(-1)^{|A| + 1}}{\prod_{p \in A} p}
   &=\left(\sum_{p \in T_N}\frac{1}{p}- \sum_{\substack{p_1,p_2 \in T_N  p_1 \neq p_2}}\frac{1}{p_1\cdot p_2}+\sum_{\substack{p_1,p_2, p_3 \in T_N  p_1 \neq p_2 \neq p_3}}\frac{1}{p_1\cdot p_2 \cdot p_3}-\dots\right)
   &= 1-\prod_{p \in T_N}\left(1-\frac{1}{p}\right).
   \end{align*}
   From here, note that for obvious reasons, \[1-\left(1-\prod_{p \in T_N}\left(1-\frac{1}{p}\right)\right) =  \prod_{p \in T_N}\left(1-\frac{1}{p}\right)\ge a_1\] when  $N \ge 2$ . Sending  $N \to \infty$  implies  $a_1 = 0$ . In fact for any  $a_k$ , omitting the prime factors of  $k$  from the product does not stop it from vanishing, so  $a_k = 0$  for all  $k \ge 1$ , which is clearly a contradiction.

