Bezout's Lemma  :love:**Solution.**Since we are given  $\gcd(m,n)=1$ . It follows that  $\exists a,b$  such that  $am+bn=1$  from [Bezout Lemma](https://brilliant.org/wiki/bezouts-identity/). Now notice the following: 
\begin{align*}
x &\equiv x^{am+bn} \pmod{p} 
&\equiv (x^{ma})(x^{bn}) \pmod{p} 
&\equiv (y^{na})(x^{bn}) \pmod{p} 
&\equiv (y^ax^b)^n \pmod{p} 
\end{align*}
Analogously, going other way around, we get  $y \equiv (y^ax^b)^m$ . Therefore,  $z \equiv y^ax^b$  works. Now to show that this  $z$  is unique. Let there  $\exists z,z'$  which satisfies. Now   $$ z^n \equiv (z')^n \pmod{p} \implies \left(\frac{z}{z'}\right)^n \equiv 1 \pmod{p} $$   whence we get  $\operatorname{ord}_p(\tfrac{z}{z'}) \mid n$ . Similarily  $\operatorname{ord}_p(\tfrac{z}{z'}) \mid m$  which means  $\operatorname{ord}_p(\tfrac{z}{z'}) \mid \gcd(m,n)=1$ . Consequently, we get  $z \equiv z'$  which finishes the problem.  $\blacksquare$

Cool problem. I didn't know Bezout's Lemma was actually of some use until now. Using the existence of inverses we can just turn off our brains and trust the algebra. We first show existance.

The edge case where  $x\equiv y \equiv 0 \pmod{p}$  is clear since then  $z \equiv 0$  is clearly a unique solution. We assume that  $x,y \not \equiv 0 \pmod{p}$  in what follows.

By Bezout's Lemma, we know there exists integers  $k$  and  $l$  such that  $km+ln = \gcd(m,n)=1$ . Thus, 
\begin{align*}
x^m & \equiv y^n \pmod{p}
x^{km} & \equiv y^{kn} \pmod{p}
x^{km+ln} & \equiv x^{ln} \cdot y^{kn} \pmod{p}
x & \equiv (x^ly^k)^n \pmod{p}
\end{align*}
A similar calculation yeilds, 
\[y \equiv (x^ly^k)^m \pmod{p}\]
which implies that  $z \equiv x^ly^k \pmod{p}$  is indeed a solution.

To see why  $z$  must be unique  $\pmod{p}$ , assume there exists  $z_1,z_2$  both which satisfy the given relations. Then, 
\[z_1^n \equiv x \equiv z_2^n \pmod{p}\]
and 
\[z_1^m \equiv y \equiv z_2^m \pmod{p}\]
Thus, 
\[\left(\frac{z_1}{z_2}\right)^m \equiv \left(\frac{z_1}{z_2}\right)^n \pmod{p}\]
But then,  $\text{ord}_p\left(\frac{z_1}{z_2}\right) \mid m$  and  $\text{ord}_p\left(\frac{z_1}{z_2}\right) \mid n$ . But since  $\gcd(m,n)=1$  this requires,  $z_1 \equiv z_2 \pmod{p}$  which proves uniqueness.

