Let  $J \equiv \overline{I_AE} \cap \overline{BC}$ . Also let  $\overline{AC}$  intersect  $\odot(DKE)$  at  $P$  other than  $E$ . We present the following small claim. 
<span style="color:#f00">**Claim.**</span>We have,  $D,B,C$  collinear.
*Proof.*In  $\triangle I_AAD$  we have   $$ \angle IAD+\angle IDA+\angle C=180^\circ \implies 2\angle IAD = 180^\circ - \angle C \implies \angle IAD = 90^\circ  - \frac{\angle C}{2} $$   but  $\angle IAD = \angle A/2+ \angle BAD$ . This implies  $\angle BAD = \angle B/2$  which again implies  $\angle BDA = \angle B/2$  which finally yields the collinearty of  $D,B,C$  as required.  $\square$ Now, note that  $\angle EJC = 90^\circ - \angle C$  and  $CE = s-b$  which implies  $CJ = \tfrac{s-b}{\cos C }$ . Also  $\angle PDJ = \angle PEJ = \angle CEJ = 90^\circ$  implying that the center of  $\odot(DKE)$  which is  $H,$  is just the midpoint of  $PJ$ . Let  $N,L$  be the foot of perpendiculars from  $H,I_A$  respectively onto  $\overline{BC}$ . Let  $M$  be the midpoint of  $BC$ . It suffices to show that   $$ \frac{NM}{LM} = \frac{HN}{I_AL}~(\clubsuit) $$   to prove the collinearty of  $H,M,I_A$ . Now   $$ NM = NJ-MJ= \frac{1}{2} \cdot DJ - (MC+CJ) = \frac{c+a+\frac{s-b}{\cos C}}{2}-\frac{a}{2}-\frac{s-b}{\cos C} = \frac{c-\frac{s-b}{\cos C}}{2} $$   and  $ML=\tfrac{a}{2}-(s-b)= \tfrac{b-c}{2}$ . Also   $$ HN = \frac{PD}{2} = \frac{(c+a) \cdot \tan C}{2} $$   while  $I_AL = r_A$ . Now  $(\clubsuit)$  is just   $$ \frac{\left(c-\frac{s-b}{\cos C}\right)/2}{(b-c)/2}=\frac{(c+a) \cdot \tan C}{2r_A} $$  To prove this statement is true, we calculate parts of it separately,   $$ \tan C= \frac{2\cdot \tan(C/2)}{1-\tan^2 (C/2)}=\frac{2 \cdot \sqrt{\frac{(s-a)(s-b)}{s(s-c)}}}{1-\frac{(s-a)(s-b)}{s(s-c)}}=\frac{2 \cdot \Delta}{2s(s-c) - ab} $$   and   $$ \cos C = 2 \cos^2 (C/2) - 1 = \frac{2s(s-c)}{ab} - 1 = \frac{2s(s-c) - ab}{ab} $$  Now  $(\clubsuit)$  is true iff 
\begin{align*}
\frac{c - \frac{s-b}{\cos C}}{b-c} &= \frac{(c+a) \cdot \frac{2 \cdot \Delta}{2s(s-c) - ab}}{2 \cdot \frac{\Delta}{s-a}} 
\iff \frac{c[2s(s-c) - ab]-(s-b) \cdot ab}{b-c} &= (c+a) \cdot (s-a) 
\iff sac+\cancel{sbc}+sc^2-ac^2-bc^2-c^3 - \cancel{abc} - sab + ab^2 &= sab - a^2b + \cancel{sbc} - \cancel{abc} - sca + a^2c - sc^2 + ac^2
\iff 2sac - bc^2 - c^3 &= 2sab-a^2b-ab^2+a^2c-2sc^2+2ac^2 
\iff a^2c + \cancel{abc} + ac^2 - bc^2 - \cancel{c^3} &= \cancel{a^2b + ab^2} + \cancel{abc} - \cancel{a^2b - ab^2} + a^2c - (a+b+\cancel{c})c^2 + 2ac^2 
\iff \cancel{a^2c} + ac^2 - bc^2 &= \cancel{a^2c} - ac^2 - bc^2 + 2ac^2 
\iff ac^2 - bc^2 &= ac^2 - bc^2 
\end{align*}
which is true. This establishes  $(\clubsuit)$  which shows that  $H,M,I_A$  are collinear, as desired.  $\blacksquare$</details>

