The game will end in a draw. Label the first player **<span style="color:#ff9a00">A</span>** (vertical) and the second player <span style="color:#00f">**B**</span> (horizontal). We provide a strategy for player <span style="color:#00f">**B**</span> to prevent **<span style="color:#ff9a00">A</span>** from getting more than  $2$  points while ensuring  $2$  points itself. We will use a  $11\times 11$  grid for the sake of demonstration. Consider the following division of the gird: 

[asy]
add(grid(11,11,gray+linetype("4 4")));
int i;
for(i=0; i<12; i=i+1)
{
draw((i,1)--(i,11));
}
int i;
for(i=0; i<6; i=i+1)
{
draw((0,2i+1)--(11,2i+1));
}
draw((0,0)--(11,0));
draw((0,0)--(0,1));
draw((11,0)--(11,1));
[/asy]
Whenever **<span style="color:#ff9a00">A</span>** draws a vertical segment in one of the dominoes <span style="color:#00f">**B**</span> should draw a horizontal segment in the other cell of the domino. In this way, it is impossible for **<span style="color:#ff9a00">A</span>** to get  $3$  vertical markings in a row. Then when **<span style="color:#ff9a00">A</span>** plays first in the bottom row, <span style="color:#00f">**B**</span> should play in a cell in the bottom row at least  $2$  away. Thus when **<span style="color:#ff9a00">A</span>** plays in the bottom row again, <span style="color:#00f">**B**</span> is guaranteed a  $2$  in a row.

The strategy for **<span style="color:#ff9a00">A</span>** to get  $2$  points while preventing <span style="color:#00f">**B**</span> from getting  $2$  points is almost identical. Thus each player can make sure the other does not win, the definition of a draw!

