<span style="color:#f00">**<u>Claim.</u>**</span>  $KE$  and  $LF$  are paralell.
*Proof.* We're going to do this by cartesian coordinates. Define \begin{align*} D=(0,0)  A=(0,a)  B=(b,0)  C=(c,0)  M=(0,m) \end{align*} Thus, its not hard to get \begin{align*} AB: \frac{-a}b \textbf{x}+a=\textbf{y}  AC: \frac{-a}c \textbf{x}+a=\textbf{y}  ME:\frac{b}a \textbf{x}+m=\textbf{y}  MF: \frac{c}a \textbf{x}+m= \textbf{y} \end{align*} Hence we calculate \begin{align*} E=(\frac{ab(a-m)}{a^2+b^2},\frac{a(b^2+am)}{a^2+b^2})  F=(\frac{ac(a-m)}{a^2+c^2},\frac{a(c^2+am)}{a^2+c^2})  P=(b,\frac{bc}a+m)  R=(c,\frac{bc}a+m)  Q=(-b,m-\frac{bc}a)  S=(-c,m-\frac{bc}a) \end{align*} From here we can get \begin{align*} SB: (\frac{bc-am}{a(b+c)}) \textbf{x}+\frac{b(am-bc)}{a(b+c)}= \textbf{y}  QC: (\frac{bc-am}{a(b+c)}) \textbf{x}+\frac{c(am-bc)}{a(b+c)}= \textbf{y} \end{align*} And then \begin{align*} K=(0,\frac{c(am-bc)}{a(b+c)})  L =(0,\frac{b(am-bc)}{a(b+c)}) \end{align*} Now, it just lefts us to check.   $$ KE: \textbf{Ax}+\textbf{B}=\textbf{y} $$     $$ K \in KE \Rightarrow \textbf{B}=\frac{c(am-bc)}{a(b+c)} $$     $$ E \in KE \Rightarrow \textbf{A}\frac{ab(a-m)}{a^2+b^2} +\frac{c(am-bc)}{a(b+c)} = \frac{a(b^2+am)}{a^2+b^2} \Rightarrow \textbf{A}=\frac{a^2(b^2+am)(b+c)-c(a^2+b^2)(am-bc)}{a(b+c)ab(a-m)} $$     $$ \textbf{A}=\frac{(a^2b^2+b^2c^2+c^2a^2)+a^2bc+a^3m-abcm}{a^2(b+c)(a-m)} $$   Which is symetric in  $B$  and  $C$ , completing the proof.  $\blacksquare$ Now, define  $X=KE\cap PQ$ ,  $Y=LF\cap RS$ ,  $U=PQ\cap SL$  and  $V=RS\cap KQ$ . Note that, by the claim, it is sufficient to show that  $ESUX$  and  $FQVY$  are both cyclic. But, by the power of  $M$ , note that we just need to show that  $XYVU$  is cyclic too, i.e.   $$ \frac{MX}{MV}=\frac{MY}{MU} $$   Which we'll do by trig. define   $$ \alpha =\angle KXF=\angle LFX $$     $$ \beta = \angle XKM=\angle YLM $$     $$ \gamma =\angle VKM=\angle SLM $$     $$ \theta =\angle MVK = \angle MSL $$   and  $x=\angle KEM$ ,.  $y=\angle KQM$ , thus, by law of sines we get   $$ \frac{MX}{MV}=\frac{\sin \beta \sin \theta}{\sin \alpha \sin \gamma}=\frac{MF}{MS} $$     $$ \frac{MF}{MS}=\frac{MF}{MR}=\frac{ME}{MP}=\frac{ME}{MQ} $$     $$ \frac{ME}{MQ}=\frac{\sin \beta \sin y}{\sin x \sin \gamma} = \frac{MY}{MU} $$   Ending the problem  :stretcher:

